  "markdown_content": "## Meeting overview\\n\\n> Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said that Iran would be the top item on the agenda for his meeting with US President Donald Trump.\\n\\nThe statement emphasized continued Israeli concern over Tehran\u2019s nuclear program and broader regional behavior, which have long been central to Israel\u2019s security calculus.\\n\\n## Agenda and objectives\\n\\nNetanyahu framed the meeting as an opportunity to coordinate policy and enhance information sharing. He and his aides described the talks as likely to cover:\\n\\n- enforcement of existing sanctions\\n- methods to counter Iran\u2019s network of regional proxies\\n- possible cooperative defensive measures to deter escalation across the Middle East\\n\\n## Analysts\u2019 views\\n\\nAnalysts said the meeting underscores the enduring importance of US-Israel strategic ties on matters of regional security. For Israel, close alignment with the United States remains central to efforts to pressure Iran diplomatically and economically, while Washington\u2019s responses will be watched for implications for sanctions policy and potential military contingencies.\\n\\n## Official briefings\\n\\nOfficial briefings ahead of the meeting were limited. Spokespeople from both sides indicated that details would be released after consultations, and neither side provided a full agenda or a timetable for any specific actions.\\n\\nObservers noted that statements from leaders can be aimed both at international partners and domestic audiences, shaping expectations about the scope of cooperation.\\n\\n## International attention and next steps\\n\\nThe conversation between the two leaders is likely to be followed closely by regional governments and international institutions monitoring Iran\u2019s activities. How the meeting translates into concrete measures will depend on subsequent diplomatic steps, intelligence assessments, and broader calculations by the United States and its partners.",
